## Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline nanoporous materials formed by the orderly installation of metal clusters and organic ligands through coordination bonds [1,2]. The diverse topologies and functionalities of chemical components endow MOFs with tunable properties and adaptable applications [3-9]. For instance, MOFs emerge as a promising platform for drug delivery and theragnostic nanomedicines [10-15]. However, as non-biological materials, the stability or the programmed instability of MOFs in physiological environment is important consideration for  their  medical applications [l6-22]. In physiological environment and in some biological culture media, amino acids (AA) are present in large quantities for sustaining the activities of living organisms [23]. MOF nanoparticles and composites are post-modified and stored in amino acid buffer solutions in different drug delivery systems [10,24,25]. Recent studies show that amino acids can be decorated into the pores of MOF [26] and can modulate the growth of MOF crystals [27,28]. There could be diverse structural changes when different MOFs interact with amino acids.
UiO-66 is a special type of MOF composed of zirconium oxide clusters and 1,4-benzene-dicarboxylate (BDC) linkers [29]. UiO66 has been utilized as a drug carrier system for delivering DNA and treating tumor and inflammation [11,30-34]. UiO-66 is regarded as a highly biocompatible and inert material that has little effect on cellular viability [35,36]. In addition, studies also show UiO-66 can survive temperatures up to $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ as a solid or in solutions with high acidity/basicity [37-39]. Its stability outperforms many other MOF systems. However,a few recent studies show that UiO-66 does degrade in certain buffer solutions and biological culture media, such as N-ethylmorpholine (NEM) and Dulbecco's modified eagle medium (DMEM) [22,40]. UiO66 gradually releases terephthalate linkers and zirconium and finally decomposes as revealed by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) [41]. The buffer solutions contain high concentrations of ions and small molecular nutrients including various amino acids. Whether the amino acids play a role in the degradation of UiO-66 is a crucial question for the medical and biological applications of UiO-66.
In this work, we investigated the stability of UiO-66 in the solutions of twenty different amino acids. We found that the amino acids can induce the amorphization of UiO-66 and leach out terephthalic ligands in basic conditions $\mathrm{(pH>~}9)\$ .Thetwo basic amino acids, lysine (Lys) and arginine (Arg), are particularly powerful in degrading UiO-66. They can replace terephthalic ligands at low concentrations. We show that the substitution effect on the zirconium clusters is strongly affected by the $\mathrm{pH}$ of the solution. We applied solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance (SSNMR) to research the structure of the degradation product of amino acids and MOF. It shows that amino acids can replace linkers and form amorphous nanoparticles with zirconium ions. The complexes of amino acids and zirconium themselves could be bio-composite materials of interesting structures and properties [42-47]. Our investigation revealed the unexpected instability of UiO-66 in the presence of amino acids and researched the degradation mechanisms. In fact, the designable degradability of MOFs is an important consideration for their biomedical applications [48-51].
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1  Synthesis of UiO-66
UiO-66 nanoparticles were synthesized according to a previously reported procedure [52]. $99.6~\mathrm{mg}$ terephthalic acid and $139.8~\mathrm{mg}$ zirconiumchlorideweredissolvedin10mLof dimethylformamide (DMF) separately. The solutions were mixed in a $30~\mathrm{mL}$ vial with $0.8~\mathrm{mL}$ formic acid and incubated in an oven at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ The resulting MOF nanoparticles were washed with DMF (repeated three times) and soaked in methanol for $24\mathrm{h}$ (repeated three times). The powder was activated at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a vacuum oven for $48\mathrm{{h}}$
## 2.2 Procedures of loading amino acid
In each batch, $\boldsymbol{0.1\mathrm{\g}}$ UiO-66 and $10\ \mathrm{mL}\ 0.1\ \mathrm{g/mL}$ amino acid solutions were mixed in a glass bottle. The mixtures were stirred at $700~\mathrm{\rpm}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting solids were separated by centrifugation and soaked in pure water for one day (repeated three times). In some batches, the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of solution was adjusted by the careful mixing of NaOH or HCl solutions. The obtained solid samples were digested with $1\mathrm{MNaOH}$ in $_{\mathrm{~D_{2}O}}$ for solution-state $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) which derived the uptake of amino acids.
## 2.3 Characterization methods
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD)measurements were carried out on a Rigaku Ultimate-IV X-ray diffractometer and operated at $40\mathrm{\kV/30\mA}$ with Cu $\mathrm{K}\alpha$ line $\mathit{\Omega}\left(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~}\right)$ .Patterns were collected in reflectance Bragg-Brentano geometry in the $2\theta$ range from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ ，
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM)images were captured on a Hitachi HT 7700 transmission electron microscope at $100~\mathrm{kV}$ and the functionalized MOF nanoparticles were deposited onto a carbon film within a copper grid. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were captured on a Hitachi S4800 scanning electron microscope.
Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA） measurements were performed on a TGA/DSC1 1100SF analyzer. Samples (ca. $5~\mathrm{mg})$ were placed in platinum crucibles. The samples were heated to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ under a continuous feed of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$
Elemental analysis (EA） was performed on Elementar Vario MICRO V1.9.7 analyzer to study the composition of the samples.
Solution-state $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra were obtained with a Bruker Advance NMR Spectrometer at resonance frequency of $400\mathrm{MHz}$ for $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ The relaxation delay $(d_{1})$ was set to $\textbf{l s}$ The number of scans was 64. Solid-state NMR experiments were performed on a $9.4~\mathrm{~T~}$ Advance III HD NMR spectrometer at resonance frequencies of 100.62 MHz for ${}^{13}\mathrm{C}.$ Magic angle spinning (MAS) experiments were performed on $3.2\mathrm{~mm}$ MAS probes at a spinning speed of $15\mathrm{kHz}$
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 The stability in amino acid solutions
After the treatment, the resulting solids were washed and dried, and then analyzed by PXRD and TEM. UiO-66 maintained its crystallinity after the treatment except those in lysine and arginine (the two basic amino acids) solutions. Some representative PXRD patterns are shown in Fig. l(a), while the rest are shown in Fig. S1 in the ESM. It clearly shows the disappearance of diffraction peaks for the two samples treated with lysine and arginine. The TEM images (Fig.1(b) and Fig.S2 in the ESM) show the fragmented morphologies of these two samples, while others still possess octahedral shapes as that of the pristine UiO-66. Aspartic acid and glutamic acid, the two representative acidic amino acids, do not affect the structure and morphology of UiO-66.
The interaction strength between amino acids and UiO-66 could be indicated by the uptake capacity of amino acids in UiO66 which was measured by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ solution-state NMR of NaOHdigested samples (Fig.2(a)). The relative concentrations of loaded amino acids are normalized with respect to BDC linkers. It shows the uptake of lysine and arginine is much higher in UiO-66 than the rest cases. The uptake could be related to the acidity/basicity of amino acid solutions as indicated by their isoelectric points (pI) (Fig.2(b)). The large pI of lysine and arginine indicate that their solutions are rather basic.
The unusually high uptake of lysine and arginine could imply the substantial replacement of BDC linkers (the degradation of UiO-66 framework) instead of the adsorption mechanism. This is supported by the TGA measurements (Fig.S3 in the ESM) as the weight percentages of organic components are greatly reduced in the samples treated with lysine and arginine. The degradation effect of UiO-66 by lysine and arginine can be induced at relatively low concentrations. According to the PXRD results (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)), UiO-66 starts to lose significant part of crystallinity when the concentration of lysine or arginine reaches $6~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ Similar phenomenon is also observed for ideal UiO-66 (Fig. S4 in the ESM). At low concentrations, the uptake of amino acids in UiO-66 grows with the concentration of amino acids in the solution (Fig.3(c)).
## 3.2 The stability under different pH values
We tested the PXRD patterns of UiO-66 after it has been soaked in amino acid solutions with different $\mathrm{pH}$ values (Figs. 4(a) and $4(\mathrm{b})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ . UiO-66 is stable in acidic solutions of amino acids when the $\mathrm{pH}$ goes down to 1. The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of digested solids shows mostly terephthalic ligands at $8\:\mathrm{ppm}$ 。suggesting a low uptake of amino acids (Fig.4(c)). However, UiO-66 turned completely amorphous when the pH reaches 9 (Fig.4(b)). The $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of digested solids also shows a significant uptake of amino acids (signals in the range of $1{-}4~\mathrm{ppm}$ )as well as the loss of BDC signals (Fig. 4(d)). This suggests the amino acids can substantially replace the BDC linkers under the basic conditions. In contrast, UiO-66 can maintain its crystalline framework under $\mathrm{\pH}$ from 1 to 13 in simple aqueous solutions (Fig.S5 in the ESM). When amino acids are present, its stability in basic solution is significantly impaired.
This $\mathrm{\pH}$ dependence is universal for all tested amino acids (Figs. S6 and S7 in the ESM),including lysine and arginine. The degradation is also observed when UiO-66 is soaked in amine or diamine solutions (l,2-diaminopropane, n-butylamine, ethylenediamine, and 1,5-diaminopentane) when the pH reaches 9 (Fig.S8 in the ESM). The instability of UiO-66 could be attributed to the liability of ligand dissociation within its structures. In fact, ligand exchange is commonly used strategy to replace BDC ligands with other functionalized ligands in UiO-66 [53,54]. Basic solution tends to accelerate the ligand exchange process and increase the substitution of BDC with amino acids or other molecules with active coordination groups.
## 3.3 Chemical composition of degraded products
In fact, direct MOF products formed by amino acids and zirconium are rare except a few reported cases [55,56]. As we show in the previous sections, amino acids can replace the linkers of UiO-66 and form amorphous nanoparticles. It is of great interest to know the chemical composition of these products. Even without a crystaline structure, the nanoparticles could still be useful for biomedical or other applications. Meanwhile, this research could give a hint about the possible chemical changes of MOFs in a real biological environment.
Here we studied reaction products of lysine and UiO-66 as an example. The reaction products in lysine solutions of concentrations from 6 to $150~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ were collected and tested by elemental analysis, TGA,and solid-state NMR. The elemental analysis shows lysine completely replaces terephthalic linkers at a concentration of $30~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ . The molar quantities of lysine and zirconium reach a relatively constant ratio of 1:4 (Fig. 5(a)). This indicates the formation of a complex composite of lysine and zirconium oxide clusters. This is in general agreement with the TGA measurements (Fig.S9 in the ESM). The SEM images show that zirconium-lysine complex grows from aggregated clusters into rod-like shapes as the lysine concentration increases (Fig. S10 in the ESM).
$^{13}\mathrm{C}$ solid-state NMR of the zirconium-lysine complex suggests lysine coordinates tozirconiummetalswithdifferent conformations (Fig.5(b)). The 175 ppm peak is the same as the shift of pure lysine, suggesting the carboxylate group is free. This species could be attributed to the lysine molecules coordinated to zirconium with the amino groups. The species at $183~\mathrm{ppm}$ has a downfield shift and can be attributed to the molecules coordinated to zirconium directly with carboxylate. The upfield shift of 167 ppm is attributed to the formation of poly-lysine (Fig.5(c)) [57]. We postulate that the abundant zirconium sites in UiO-66 and the basic condition could catalyze the coupling reactions between amino acids.
## 4 Conclusions
In this work, we found that amino acids can significantly impair the stability of UiO-66 when $\mathrm{\pH}\geq9$ . The degradation effect can be attributed to the accelerated exchange process between ligand and amino acids under basic conditions. The basic lysine and arginine have stronger degradation effect than other amino acids. We also investigated the composition of degradation products in which amino acids replace organic linkers and form amino acidzirconium complexes. Our research provides a new understanding of the possible chemical evolutions of UiO-66 for biomedical applications. The formation of amino acid-zirconium complexes could be interesting for the design of new bio-composite materials [58-61].
